GNU tar 1.10 on OS-9/68000 -- changes made by this collection=============================================================The port itself came with the tree: every `#ifdef OSK' in these sources,rmdir.c's new_chown and new_chmod, and the older copies in OLD/ are theporter's.  ORIG/ holds each file below as it stood before thiscollection touched it.  Every change is inside `#ifdef OSK'.2026-09-24: THE OWNER AND GROUP, recorded and restored rightOS-9 keeps a file's owner as one word, group then user: a file ownedby 1.7 is group 1, user 7.  tar keeps it as uid and gid, and as thenames uname and gname.  Two things were wrong.1. Recording.  Built with this collection's recipe, gtar recorded every   file as su/0.  os9lib.l's stat fills in everything but st_uid and   st_gid -- the same fault `ls -l' had until 2026-09-23.   rmdir.c    new os9_owner(): opens the path with no access bits,              which asks RBF for no permission, reads the file              descriptor with _gs_gfd, and sets st_gid from FD_OWN's              first byte and st_uid from its second.  A directory is              opened with the directory bit.   create.c   dump_file calls os9_owner after its stat, so the header              gets uid 7, gid 1 and uname `tester'.   diffarch.c do_stat does the same, so `gtar d' compares real owners.2. Reading back.  decode_header, in list.c, took the owner of a   standard archive from uname and gname alone.  gname is always empty   here -- there is no SYS/group -- so listing, which never set the   numbers at all, printed a group of 0 for every file, and an extract   fell back to getgid(): as the super-user a file owned 1.7 came back   31.7, and one owned 0.0 came back 31.0.   list.c     decode_header takes st_uid and st_gid from the header's              numeric fields first.  A uname or gname that this              system's password or group file knows still wins, as in              the original; one it does not know keeps the number.              Includes <pwd.h> and <grp.h> for the two lookups.Measured on this disk, a file owned 1.7 and one owned 0.0, archivedand listed by tester and extracted by the super-user:    before   tvf: tester/0, su/0     extract: 31.7, 31.0    after    tvf: tester/1, su/0     extract:  1.7,  0.0tools/datatests/gtarown.cases asserts both.  An extract by anyone butthe super-user leaves the files owned by whoever extracted them, astar always has.  Directories an extract makes are not given theirrecorded owner on OS-9; that `#ifndef OSK' is the porter's and isunchanged.